{:edb/db-before-t 3756 :edb/db-after-t 3757 :edb/tx-data [[13194139537069 50 #inst "2026-10-18T03:49:21.402Z" 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278208 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<1-2x\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=1-2x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278208 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<1-2x\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=1-2x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(-1,3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\n- **II.** The point $P(-1,3)$ belongs to $A$\n\n- **III.** The point $Q(1,-1)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278208 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=1-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ce1ca6af3caf2b454d7fed484f98d11478839bcf107c0f19562688c7cdaca6a.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-1,3)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/aa/aad8bc7bfd64af0dd1e08618626ae3237bb4aba42be1f91fe7ff9328ad6ecbe7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ satisfy $y=1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278208 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=1-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ce1ca6af3caf2b454d7fed484f98d11478839bcf107c0f19562688c7cdaca6a.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-1,3)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/aa/aad8bc7bfd64af0dd1e08618626ae3237bb4aba42be1f91fe7ff9328ad6ecbe7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(-1,3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ satisfy $y=1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $P(-1,3)$ belongs to $A$” is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $Q(1,-1)$ is an interior point of $A$” is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“The point $P(-1,3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” only.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278218 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x>1\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278218 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x>1\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(1,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\n- **II.** The point $P(1,1)$ belongs to $A$\n\n- **III.** The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278218 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the right of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7dc932c43ed153f4ac1832ca136f9035d879ea1f54e01c69e7a032b7a53d4940.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/06/06fdba4e26ebad28ef3f63106b7bdd8cb115b91a5f0076370a27b12f05added3.png)\n\n \n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ satisfy $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $x>1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278218 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the right of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7dc932c43ed153f4ac1832ca136f9035d879ea1f54e01c69e7a032b7a53d4940.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/06/06fdba4e26ebad28ef3f63106b7bdd8cb115b91a5f0076370a27b12f05added3.png)\n\n \n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(1,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ satisfy $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $P(1,1)$ belongs to $A$” is false. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $x>1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$” is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“The point $P(1,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” only.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278219 1071 "![](images/5d/5dccdb1f8957bb15d7b2215320c6e632819a647bf4dc350ff850876bae6298ec.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-3,2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278219 1071 "![](images/5d/5dccdb1f8957bb15d7b2215320c6e632819a647bf4dc350ff850876bae6298ec.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-3,2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278219 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/d5/d595e29d31743694c404b99c79c60e40b152d30083e633d4269ad4444be3f826.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/23/2336ff8a65dd397e8324384b9f9536f3c7d4ad2d76a81caeba28d7fe3ec229ef.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/26/26903667d45cecf81539025c4cda333129cf89600821b383f20d574c75e537b0.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278219 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$” is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/d5/d595e29d31743694c404b99c79c60e40b152d30083e633d4269ad4444be3f826.png)\n\n- Statement “$B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$” is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/23/2336ff8a65dd397e8324384b9f9536f3c7d4ad2d76a81caeba28d7fe3ec229ef.png)\n\n- Statement “$C$ is an interior point of the set $M$” is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/26/26903667d45cecf81539025c4cda333129cf89600821b383f20d574c75e537b0.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“$A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$”, “$B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$”, and “$C$ is an interior point of the set $M$”.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278220 1071 "![](images/73/73f61de1a716545449439ed8875b465dc5aec6f3148e520ad028a96725f2a4f0.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[0,1]\\cup {2}$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278220 1071 "![](images/73/73f61de1a716545449439ed8875b465dc5aec6f3148e520ad028a96725f2a4f0.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[0,1]\\cup {2}$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278220 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained in the set $M.$\n\n![](images/84/84afb5331ef9244120d9f3d9b14e25fa2d19da31f8eb685c5d74b089e0d99289.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/c0/c0b35fafc6e628c36e41fa8df42e3a477d8aac4af0b4c3819fa0063e5bd014df.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $C$ is a point of $M$ and $C$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $C.$\n\n![](images/e5/e54fa85543a79c18e00d3b03281d6774c98fdb03e9b5440b8f179ce5a0d09519.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278220 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is an interior point of the set $M$” is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained in the set $M.$\n\n![](images/84/84afb5331ef9244120d9f3d9b14e25fa2d19da31f8eb685c5d74b089e0d99289.png)\n\n- Statement “$B$ is an interior point of the set $M$” is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/c0/c0b35fafc6e628c36e41fa8df42e3a477d8aac4af0b4c3819fa0063e5bd014df.png)\n\n- Statement “$C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$” is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $C$ is a point of $M$ and $C$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $C.$\n\n![](images/e5/e54fa85543a79c18e00d3b03281d6774c98fdb03e9b5440b8f179ce5a0d09519.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“$A$ is an interior point of the set $M$” and “$C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$” only.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278221 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{y}^{2}\\ge -2x},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{y}^{2}=-2x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278221 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{y}^{2}\\ge -2x},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{y}^{2}=-2x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(-2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(-2,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278221 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the right of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e5547cb8e0ed0fec1fd2e515a1bcd9009044289dfdc516c6a6cba14b650a7645.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-2,2)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7b/7b353dd43fd293424ba8404184e53869d41d8a1b73493d845bc85aa333130606.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the equation ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${y}^{2}\\ge -2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278221 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the right of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e5547cb8e0ed0fec1fd2e515a1bcd9009044289dfdc516c6a6cba14b650a7645.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-2,2)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7b/7b353dd43fd293424ba8404184e53869d41d8a1b73493d845bc85aa333130606.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(-2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the equation ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(-2,2)$ belongs to $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${y}^{2}\\ge -2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$” is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“The point $P(-2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” and “The point $P(-2,2)$ belongs to $A$” only.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278222 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278222 1071 "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\n- **II.** The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$\n\n- **III.** The point $Q(3,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278222 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/18/181a21e9975320e54365ef10bd801a2fa6494058dd40cda79cb29b713bd8df84.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(3,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/15/15d42de861a3e687a269326e8338ea5d99b6a5a609f4a20e597e64ccc4a8474a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ do not satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278222 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/18/181a21e9975320e54365ef10bd801a2fa6494058dd40cda79cb29b713bd8df84.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(3,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/15/15d42de861a3e687a269326e8338ea5d99b6a5a609f4a20e597e64ccc4a8474a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” is false. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ do not satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $Q(3,3)$ is an interior point of $A$” is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$” only'." 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278224 1071 "![](images/07/07463b409d54d1970ee9d47cad5de7188ea44e682a58e7e941b3cd4bf7f5efa6.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-1,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278224 1071 "![](images/07/07463b409d54d1970ee9d47cad5de7188ea44e682a58e7e941b3cd4bf7f5efa6.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-1,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537069 17592186576411 true] [17592186278224 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside $M.$\n\n![](images/73/734ff08a25e25486dc65f919ce99bc5422ca8ce264bb7d044479b1eb3b6a716b.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/28/286f8b397b3c908d29678640d691558a784e31751d09c68e6b6da855c272231c.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/7e/7ed7726fa1a139ded4a9226bcb01c17d0ad46ef4bd03a820f033640c68778453.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'" 13194139537069 17592186576411 false] [17592186278224 1074 "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f88